{
  "dom_id": "step-q27000",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)+1$ by $1$ unit *down.* \n\nHence, if the point $(-3,1)$ lies on $y=f(x)+1$, then the point \n\n$$\n(-3,1-1)=(-3,0)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/2c76b54e0dc12d52fbc6e41a601d5e87a105537e055a9d4cee4c758b0dd3ae63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(-3,1)$ lies on $y=f(x)+1$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3)+1 & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =1-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=-3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(-3,0)$ must lie on $y=f(x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
